{"kind":"request","text":"A red `make spike` run does not mean a security property broke, and the evidence explaining what did happen is captured and then discarded. Replaces 0ddc6387, which asked for the failing case to be named — usually there is not one.\n\nMeasured, not inferred. I ran spike/cmd/gitseq-report four times on an unloaded machine: runs 1 and 3 failed, runs 2 and 4 passed. In both failing runs every one of the six cases carried status `pass` in the written evidence. So the spike fails roughly half the time here, and when it does, nothing in its own record says a case failed.\n\nThe cause is at spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go:129. `report.Status` is set to fail when `runErr != nil || scanner.Err() != nil` — when the underlying test process errors or its output cannot be scanned — regardless of case results. That is a reasonable thing to want, but it collapses two events with different meanings into one word. \"One of the six adversarial security properties failed\" and \"the harness died before it could tell us\" both print `fail` and exit 1. The first should stop everything; the second should be investigated as a flaky harness. Today they are indistinguishable, and the tempting reading of a red run — spike is flaky again — is the one that would hide a real regression.\n\nThe explanation is not missing, it is hidden. Line 96 captures the test runner's stderr into a buffer; line 202 writes it into .spike/SPIKE-RESULTS.md under a \"Test runner stderr\" heading. It is never printed. CI runs `make spike` with no artifact upload, so that file lives on the runner until the job ends and is then deleted. The diagnosis is written down and thrown away, every time.\n\nTwo consequences that make this worth more than a flaky-test ticket. Runs 31382170054 and 31382676880 both failed on README-only commits — 3c6d676c changed four lines, c6128ff1 sixteen — so CI has already been red twice for reasons no one can now reconstruct. And the six cases exist to establish security properties the repository publicly claims; a gate that cannot say whether the property or the harness failed is not establishing much.\n\nI have not diagnosed the underlying intermittent yet. Capturing the hidden stderr from a failing run is the obvious next step and is in progress; whoever takes this should not assume my characterisation of the root cause, only the measurements above.","body":{"conditions":"A failing `make spike` distinguishes a case failure from a harness failure in what it prints and in the written evidence, so a reader can tell whether a security property broke or the runner died; the captured test-runner stderr reaches the console on failure rather than only the file, so a CI log alone carries the diagnosis; the evidence files survive a failing CI run by artifact upload or another durable route, with the retention window chosen deliberately since they describe adversarial cases; the underlying intermittent is diagnosed and either fixed or recorded as a known defect with its cause named, rather than left as an unexplained fifty-percent failure rate; a passing run is no noisier than today; demonstrated by forcing each of the two failure modes separately and reading the resulting output; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}